\title{Sign Misattribution in Step-Independent Agent RL:\\Dual Mechanism Diagnosis and Episode-Level Fix}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Multi-turn agent reinforcement learning requires credit assignment across long interaction trajectories, yet current frameworks default to step-independent windowing that decomposes trajectories into independently optimized windows. We show this introduces a fundamental credit assignment pathology driven by two mechanisms: \textit{policy information loss}, which truncates context and imposes a constant performance ceiling, and \textit{advantage sign misattribution}, which causes gradient updates to reflect episode outcomes rather than local action quality, producing cumulative degradation. At a baseline success rate of $p{=}12.5\%$ with group size $K{=}8$, approximately 80.9\% of gradient updates for locally correct actions carry incorrect sign---consistent with an empirically measured 52\% credit agreement rate indistinguishable from random chance. A $2{\times}2$ factorial experiment separates the mechanisms: eliminating sign misattribution converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning; further removing information loss enables generalization. Episode-level Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), which treats the full trajectory as a single optimization sample, simultaneously resolves both mechanisms. However, a systematic evaluation across 11 random seeds reveals that stable training is necessary but not sufficient: 73\% of seeds avoid collapse, but only 27\% achieve statistically significant OOD generalization ($p{<}0.01$; 25--31\% OOD vs.\ 6.7\% SFT baseline). Cross-scale verification at 8B confirms both failure and fix (90.3\% OOD vs.\ collapse to 1.5\%), and cross-environment validation on ScienceWorld confirms the collapse generalizes beyond ALFWorld.
\end{abstract}

\input{introduction}
\input{method}
\input{theoretical_analysis}
\input{experiments}
\input{related_work}
\input{discussion}
\input{conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our experiments span two environments (ALFWorld, ScienceWorld) and two scales (1.7B, 8B); additional environments and scales would strengthen generality. The 8B and ScienceWorld results report single-seed runs due to computational constraints. Among 11 seeds at 1.7B, only 27\% achieve statistically significant OOD generalization; we cannot rule out that stagnant seeds would eventually generalize with substantially more training steps, though seed100's minimal improvement from step~5 to step~10 suggests the bottleneck is not simply training duration. The mechanism driving seed-dependent transfer remains an open question. For very long episodes ($T > 100$) or tasks with many independent sub-goals, uniform episode-level advantage may limit efficiency even though it preserves direction correctness.

